WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:07.899
I’m sure you’re well-familiar with the
whole pi vs. tau debate.

00:00:07.899 --> 00:00:11.340
Many people say that the fundamental circle
constant we hold up should be the ratio of

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:16.930
a circle’s circumference to its radius,
around 6.28, not the ratio to its diameter,

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:20.099
the more familiar 3.14.

00:00:20.099 --> 00:00:25.230
These days we call this larger constant “tau”,
popularized by Michael Hartl’s “Tau manifesto”,

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:28.869
although personally, I’m quite partial to
Robert Palais’ proposed notation of a pi

00:00:28.869 --> 00:00:30.240
with three legs.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:35.730
In this manifesto and many other places on
the internet, you can read to no end about

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:40.540
how many formulas look at lot cleaner using
tau, largely because the number of radians

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:45.750
describing a given fraction of a circle is
actually that fraction of tau.

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:48.909
That dead horse is beat, I’m not here to
make the case further.

00:00:48.909 --> 00:00:53.390
Instead I’d like to talk about the seminal
moment in history when pi as we know it became

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:54.820
the standard.

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:58.629
For this, one fruitful place to look is at
the old notes and letters by one of history’s

00:00:58.629 --> 00:01:02.010
most influential mathematicians, Leonhard
Euler.

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:07.159
Luckily, we now have an official 3blue1brown
Switzerland correspondent Ben Hambrecht who

00:01:07.159 --> 00:01:10.799
was able to go to the library in Euler’s
hometown and get his hands on some of these

00:01:10.799 --> 00:01:12.890
original documents.

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:18.600
It might surprise you see Euler write (in
French), “Let pi be the circumference of

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:22.600
a circle whose radius = 1”.

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:28.689
That is, the 6.28 constant which we call tau
today, where he was likely using pi as the

00:01:28.689 --> 00:01:31.609
greek letter “p” for “perimeter”.

00:01:31.609 --> 00:01:36.600
So was it the case that Euler was more notationally
enlightened than the rest of the world?

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:38.310
Fighting the good fight for 6.28?

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:45.409
If so, who’s the villain of our story pushing
the 3.14 constant that students are shown

00:01:45.409 --> 00:01:46.409
today?

00:01:46.409 --> 00:01:50.819
The work that really established 3.14... as
the commonly recognized circle constant was

00:01:50.819 --> 00:01:53.930
an early calculus book from 1748.

00:01:53.930 --> 00:01:58.380
At the start of chapter 8, in describing the
semi-circumference of a circle with radius

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:06.021
1, and after expanding out 128 digits of the
this number, the author writes “which for

00:02:06.021 --> 00:02:09.209
the sake of brevity I may write π”.

00:02:09.209 --> 00:02:12.730
There were other texts and letters here and
there with varying conventions for the notation

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:17.450
of various circle constants, but this book,
and this section in particular, was really

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:21.980
the one to spread the notation through Europe,
and eventually the world.

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:25.640
So who wrote this text with such an unprincipled
take towards circle constants?

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:26.640
Well...Euler again.

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:33.620
In fact, we can also find instances of Euler
using the symbol pi to represent a quarter

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:37.120
turn of a circle, what we would today call
“pi/2”.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:42.810
In fact, Euler’s use of the letter pi seems
to be much more analogous to our use of the

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:44.050
greek letter theta.

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:48.980
It’s typical for us to let it represent
an angle, but no particular angle.

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:55.350
Sometimes it’s 30o, other times 135o, most
times just a variable for a general statement.

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:58.360
It depends on the problem and context before
us.

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:03.070
Likewise, Euler just let pi represent whatever
circle constant best suited the problem before

00:03:03.070 --> 00:03:04.070
him.

00:03:04.070 --> 00:03:07.680
Though it’s worth pointing out he typically
framed things in terms of unit circles, with

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:13.590
radius 1, so the 3.14 constant would have
been thought of as the ratio of a circle’s

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:19.120
semi-circumference to its radius, none of
this circumference to its diameter nonsense.

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:22.660
And I think Euler’s use of this symbol carries
with it a general lesson about how we should

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:24.000
approach to math.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:28.360
What you have to understand about Euler is
that he solved problems.

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:29.640
A lot of problems.

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:33.980
I mean, day in day out breakfast lunch and
dinner this man was thinking about puzzles,

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:39.030
formulas, having insights and creating entire
new fields left and right.

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:44.050
He wrote over 500 books and papers during
his lifetime, what amounted to about 800 pages

00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:51.240
per year, with another 400 publications appearing
posthumously.

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:55.650
It’s often joked that formulas in math have
to be named after the second person to prove

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:58.770
them, since the first person will always be
Euler.

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:02.860
His mind was not focused which circle constant
should be taken as fundamental; it was how

00:04:02.860 --> 00:04:07.870
do I solve the task sitting in front of him
and writing a letter to the Bernoulli's boasting

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:09.660
about doing so.

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:13.150
For some problems, the quarter-circle-constant
was most natural to think about.

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:21.780
For others, the full circle, and for others
still, the half circle.

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:26.139
Too often in math education the focus is on
which of multiple competing views of a topic

00:04:26.139 --> 00:04:27.689
is “right”.

00:04:27.689 --> 00:04:32.270
Is it correct to say the sum of all positive
integers -1/12, or is it correct to say it

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:34.009
diverges to infinity?

00:04:34.009 --> 00:04:38.000
Can the infinitesimals values of calculus
be taken literally, or is it only correct

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:39.690
to speak in terms of limits?

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:42.110
Are you allowed to divide a number by 0?

00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:45.289
These questions in isolation just don’t
matter.

00:04:45.289 --> 00:04:49.870
Our focus should be on specific problems and
puzzles, both those of practical application

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:53.949
and those of idle pondering for knowledge’s
own sake.

00:04:53.949 --> 00:04:59.320
When questions of standards arise, then, you
can answer them with respect to a given context.

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:04.910
Inevitably, different contexts will lend themselves
to different answers of what seems most natural,

00:05:04.910 --> 00:05:06.310
but that’s okay.

00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:11.129
Outputting 800 pages a year of transformative
insights seems to be more correlated with

00:05:11.129 --> 00:05:15.349
a flexibility towards conventions than it
does with focusing on which standards are

00:05:15.349 --> 00:05:17.139
objectively right.

00:05:17.139 --> 00:05:22.439
So on this pi day, the next time someone tells
you we should really be celebrating math on

00:05:22.439 --> 00:05:27.240
June 28th, see how quickly you can change
the topic to one where you’re talking about

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:27.939
an actual piece of math.

